{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1539180-completed-actual-play-catnap-s-carehouse","id":1539180,"title":"Completed Actual Play - Catnap's Carehouse","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Beast: The Primordial"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-07-24T19:49:04-07:00","id":1539180,"title":"Completed Actual Play - Catnap's Carehouse","content":"Part 1/2<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Chicago.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">There are plenty of folks that call it home, some upstanding, some much, much less so. But even here, in this dirt-hole masquerading as civilization, festering with the refuse of humanity, one can find individuals with qualities that set them apart from the teeming masses. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">One such individual is surrounded by medical texbooks with annotations on unexplained diseases, pill bottles with labels based on nursery rhymes, and plushies resembling venomous animals that a few orphans had taken to loving homes.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">This is the story of that man, or rather, that monster. His name is Trevor Graham, and he has come a long way from his own broken childhood to become a doctor that children (reluctantly) look up to as a guardian figure in a cold, hard, lonely world.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor considers himself a reasonable man, unlike those who don't take good care of their children. And to his conflicted satisfaction, reason has taken a dirt-nap in this city of spilled coffee, months-old tobacco, and shattered kneecaps.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">And the sleep of reason breeds monsters.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor is currently working at Saint Helen's Blessed Of The Shroud Orphanage. It is a drowsy Monday morning, and his coworkers give him a wide, fearful berth when he walks by. It has been said Trevor has cat-like eyes and smile, like he enjoys toying with people. This has a markedly different effect on the orphans, starved for attention as they are through no fault of their own. Victor, one of the orphans and notoriously violent troublemaker, has disappeared from Saint Helen 48 hours ago, and a police investigation has been initiated to find out the child's whereabouts. Trevor has been tempted to take more headache and insomnia pills than is healthy, now that he is both overworked and overworried as a result of Victor's disappearance. To add insult to injury, he has received a handwritten letter from Harley Sawyer, a Heroine and much less cherished doctor who has found special work in the forensics laboratory of Torchkeep, a detective agency of Hunters and Heroes. The letter says the following: </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u201cStay away from this case, we at Torchkeep have it under control. I know what you really are, and you disgust me. Children don't need &quot;fun-loving&quot; monsters like you, they need responsible teachers like me. I will know if you try to interfere, and I'll act accordingly.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor is handling paperwork, when he receives a phonecall from Luke, one of his Kin who is a wasp-like Changeling and who once lived at Saint Helen's orphanage. Trevor can hear Luke's buzzing, clicking voice through the transmission, while most people would hear the voice of a young boxer and rapper with anger management issues. However, this time he sounded both distressed and apologetic, like he was afraid of asking Trevor for help.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;H-h-h-h-hey uh... hey doc. I uh, I fucked up bad, I need you to fix me up. I took some weird pills at a party I shouldn't have. Heard about Victor, has he been suffering from any nightmares lately? If you fix me up, I'll get my motley to look into what happened and see if there's shit going bump in the night.&quot; </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Of course, the issue with Harley Sawyer, in particular, telling Trevor to screw off, is that this is a great way to get him interested out of spite. He was already going to look into Victor for professional reasons, though he wasn't sure if it was as rescuer or disciplinarian - but Harley? Harley needed a reminder that &quot;disciplinarian&quot; was not the same as &quot;mean girl all grown up&quot; - as well as a good way to humiliate her in her territory. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">When Luke calls however, the playful-in-all-the-worst-ways feline retreats, and the doctor comes out. &quot;Luke, Luke, it's okay. Tell me where you are, and I'll see what I can do. Let's get you first, then we'll talk about Victor.&quot; Inwardly, however, he thanks the memory he's had ever since he became the great diseased urban predator - he can actually say for certain if he thinks on it for a second.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor remembers that Victor had been suffering from recurring nightmares about being trapped in a birdcage, and trying to pick the lock only to hurt his fingers on a mechanism he couldn't understand nor break open. The nightmares always ended when a mysterious, shadowy figure appeared, unlocked the birdcage and gestured with its hand for Victor to play outside, and Victor would accept the offer, only to return to the birdcage with his hands covered in bloodstains that were not his own. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">As unneeded as it is, Trevor writes it down, a habit from his mortal days that now has use in order to collate stuff in his memories as he goes out to meet with Luke.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor meets Luke in a basketball court that is not very busy at this time. Luke is sitting on top of a tree branch, drinking from a water bottle and chewing on a protein bar. His backpack dangles from its straps on another tree branch, a large wasp-like sticker on the back. The phrase &quot;fly like a butterfly, sting like a wasp&quot; is underneath the sticker, and the smell coming from Luke is like a mixture of pine and honey cologne that does little to hide his real acrid musk from Trevor.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Before you start... I can still pull it off, you know how I get when I'm on the ground too much,&quot; He preemptively defended himself, and crawled on his six-limbs to grab his backpack. He jumps down with uncanny grace, putting the backpack around his shoulders mid-fall. &quot;Hold on, scratch that,&quot; He dashed off to vomit inside a nearby trash can, one of his protein bars falling out of his hoodie's pockets. Was that a still living larva writhing inside it?</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...I don't doubt that, but I'd expect larva to be more attached to *me,&quot;* Trevor says, picking up the bar and getting out some ipecac and wound cream. &quot;Any injuries, or is it just the drug aftereffects.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;I don't have injuries, but those pills were fucking awful. They made me hear lullabies that sounded familiar, like it was my mother singing to me. I even coulda sworn I ate slices of apple pie, but they weren't real either. Those pills gave me the best night of sleep I had in my life, but then I got nausea like a motherfucker, and the Swarm says I've been sleepwalking too. I'm worried, cuz what if I start flying and stinging people while sleepwalking? Summer Court's not gonna like that, me picking that kinda fight like a dumbass.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Yeesh. Agreed. Ipecac might work then, but right now? I'd avoid any sleep medication for a day or two, that sounds like an overdose. Though...lullabies? That's new. Sounds like my own abilities when I'm on a roll.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;I didn't find out where those pills came from, but if anybody knows, it's gotta be someone in the Spring Court. The party happened in this guy's flat, name's Yarnspinner, a rich guy who sells fancy clothes. Last time I saw him, he was sitting on a bench in Grant Park and looking at a flyer for some religious service of some sort with a worried look on his face. I tried chatting him up, but he put the flyer away and pretended that it didn't exist before telling me he had to get going.&quot; Trevor remembers that Victor once came into Saint Helen's Orphanage with a flyer that matched Luke's description. Victor had shown the flyer to Trevor a couple of times, often asking about who they were and if they could adopt him. When the police had arrived to investigate Victor's disappearance, they saw that there was a bloodstain near Victor's room, and that flyer had been torn in half and left on the floor.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...huh. That sounds...disturbingly familiar,&quot; Trevor says. &quot;Victor had a similar flier.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;That does <i>not</i> sound good. You already fixed me up, so do you want me to call the Swarm and help you look into this shady group? They might be a cult or something, and last time I checked with one of the Autumn Court guys, just about every cult is involved with supernatural shit.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Agreed. This is clearly something that is a shared target. Just, uh, look out for any detectives called Torchkeep. You know Harley Sawyer? She works with them.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;I heard about Torchkeep, but I dunno much about Harley Sawyer. She got some huge beef with you and by extension me, I'm guessing? What did you even do? In my opinion, you're helping toughen up some kids for the harsh reality that life ain't the modern kinda fairy tale, but you're not treating them like they are garden pests,&quot; There was a pause, and a world-weary sigh. &quot;That was not meant to be a pun, this isn't really a funny subject. Got anyone else in your list of contacts in the nightmareborn circles who might have info on this case?&quot; </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Honestly? It's because she and I are two sides of the same coin...and also she's an overbearing bully who regards the medical wing as an extension of high school,&quot; Trevor says, rolling her eyes. &quot;And a couple, but I haven't quite found the right brood yet. A couple aren't sure if 'children' and 'Horrors' mix...and Harvey is glowering at a lot of the rest.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor remembers two Beasts who are Kin with Changelings much like himself.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Gordon Little, an Anakim Predator who owns a famous family restaurant named The Cauldron. He's known for prizing the respectability and hospitality of his establishment, so if anyone behaved in a way that drove away the clientele, he would know and hunt them down.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Toby Taylor, an Inguma Nemesis who works at a rehabilitative art center for young adults who struggle with mental illnesses. He's like Trevor in his overbearing way of caring for kids, but differs in the sense that he prefers to inspire them to channel their psychological wounds into artworks.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...Gordon,&quot; Trevor finishes, thinking out loud. &quot;If these people are that disruptive, he is probably the first to know. I'll interview him&quot; he says, writing down contact info for Toby. &quot;And you call this number, if you're interested. Mr. Taylor is probably more than happy to ask around if Victor's involved and in danger.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Luke nods and places the contact info for Toby in his pocket. &quot;Be seeing ya soon, and try to only get into a fight when I'm around. Been hankering to bust some heads that at least know how to dodge, and the Summer Court doesn't hold fighting tournaments everyday.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor takes a taxi to The Cauldron, which is considerably busy at this time of the day. The overall atmosphere is surprisingly peaceful, with the clients chatting about their jobs, hobbies and relationships without fear of anything that might treat them as food, or be hiding in their food. People turn their heads to stare at Trevor, a few of them complaining under their breath about how his smell is ruining their appetite. But none dare look him in the eyes once he smiles at them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;See you around, and trust me, I will try not to,&quot; he says to Luke before leaving.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">To be honest, it's more the lack of smiles that bother him. He was never the most social individual, relationships beyond the professional were a bit like hard whiskey to him. To be indulged sparingly and carefully. But he used to be able to get a smile with a smile. The smell could be fought if he needed relations, but the smile? That needed more work. But in any case, he goes up to the host. &quot;Hello, is Mr. Little here? I'm an old acquaintance of his, and I think there is something he needs to know. The name is Trevor Graham.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;If you're an acquaintance with important news, you won't have to wait long,&quot; The host replied in a matter-of-fact tone with a twinge of gleeful trepidation.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">As if on cue, Trevor begins to hear a giant's thundering footsteps coming from inside the kitchen, which none of the mortal clients seem to hear.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Upon meeting gazes with Trevor, Gordon Little gives him an generously horrific smile. It would have been less unnerving if his teeth turned into the fangs of a wolf, but instead they remained mostly human-like and grew to uncanny proportions that strained against the frame of his jaw.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Gimme the latest sniff,&quot; Gordon requested in his usual eccentric form of food-related wordplay. &quot;You have been working up a sweat, and it smells of fear. I'll give you a drink on house if you let me smell deep.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Smell up,&quot; Trevor says, shrugging. &quot;One of my patients vanished, and it involves some supernatural things that might well have swept up some of out shared Kin in it. One that involves the word 'cult', and now I'm dodging my own natural enemy while trying to track him down. And now I'm wondering if they've been bad guests here too, they sound...disruptive.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Gordon scratches his beard pensively. &quot;There was one unruly man trying to get the attention of the child of another customer, but he was clearly unwell and not thinking straight. He kept falling asleep when asked for his order, and then would sing lullabies and ditties while sleepwalking. Or should I say, sleep-fidgeting. The man kept messing with his cutlery like that old knife game you play with your fingers when you're bored. I kicked him out, but made sure to ask him why he entered my establishment just to embarass himself. All he could do was give me this pamphlet with a dopey grin on his sleep-deprived face, and gave promises of <i>'new chances for the wayward'</i> before leaving,&quot; Gordon hands Trevor the pamphlet in question, which is the same type as the one Victor ripped in half inside his room before disappearing.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;I take it you are thinking the same thing I am?&quot; Gordon asks expectantly as he licks his lips. &quot;Will you have your usual drink, and if yes, will it be spicy or not? Seems to me that a frail fellow such as yourself might have to resort to less physical means of self-defense if you are sinking your teeth into the affairs of cults,&quot; This was Gordon's way of asking if Trevor wanted a drink made of Dread Fruit, which contain Satiety that Beasts can digest.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Spicy, yes. I am thinking I will need some extra kick for this...because yes, I think it is, in fact the same thing. Hoo boy.&quot;</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Gordon comes back with said drink, which is still bubbling hot and smells of carrion, embalming fluids and catnip. Whether the human eyeballs are real or made of nightmare-flesh is anyone's guess.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Having been used to this, Trevor mostly sips it annoyed he doesn't have time to enjoy the flavor. He's too busy studying the pamphlet, forward and back.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">As Trevor examines the pamphlet, he notices that the religious service is using a baking goods sale as a method of fundraising for a charitable organization focused on children, which he recognizes is legitimate but has a long history of attracting funds from supernaturals obsessed with becoming parents. Judging from the photos and names of the baking goods, along with his Ensorcellment that reveals what is hidden by fey illusions, Trevor deduces that many of the ingredients use hallucinogenics, soporifics, sedatives and Dark Mother knows what else.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">This includes a brand of storeroom tranquilizers that are no longer in production due to violating the safety parameters of the FDA. More specifically, when mixed with certain chemicals available in most kitchens and bathrooms, this brand was known to cause a temporary form of Ensorcellment in children. Those who became addicted to this substance often developed sleep disorders as teenagers and adults, almost always marked by sleepwalking and nightmares.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Seeing that, Trevor does his best to inscribe as many details from the baking goods, what he knows of the front, and the chemical mixture of the drugs to memory. This will be <i>very</i> useful in the future.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">As Trevor leaves The Cauldron, he receives a call from Luke again.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Hey doc, I spoke to that Toby guy, and he said he wants to meet you at Saint Helen's. Said he is suffering from symptoms of something that looks like a curse. His allies among the Lost don't think it's anything fey, and judging from what the Pumpkinheads told me about nightmare-born... it might have something to do with that bitchy doc with the stupid haircut who's always trying to take you down. Didn't she always talk about \u2018<i>cutting your influence out of society like a tumor\u2019?</i> Well, Toby has been running from the sight of all sharp objects against his will for a few days now. Sound familiar?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...quite. And given what I've learned, I'm not entirely sure if it's one of our curses. I'll be right there.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Upon returning to Saint Helen's, Trevor finds a cheerful young man with a clown-esque outfit, thick beard and handlebar mustache waving at him. He's sitting cross-legged on a park bench, and snacking on a box of bonbons with a finger-painted note on it saying: &quot;from Susie, for my favorite art teacher!&quot; He is carrying both an illustrator's toolkit and a guitar case, all sharing the same visuals of his psychedelic outfit.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Would you like some bonbons, Doctor Graham?&quot; Toby offers in his sing-song voice he's honed over the years of calming and motivating the unsound of mind. &quot;I have something to ask of you, and it pertains to someone both you and I know. Though you might find my request distasteful, you have a case on your hands involving Victor, no? I know certain folk who would be capable of tracking him down if he's been taken to stranger places.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Wouldn't mind, if you're offering. I probably won't be able to eat for a bit,&quot; he says, taking one. &quot;And...same. But...that isn't the only thing, is it?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;A wannabe do-gooder has put a snake in my hat, you see. Now I've been bit and it's making my life a lot more troublesome than it has to be!&quot; He reaches inside his pocket and pulls out a crayon drawing made by someone named Susie. It depicts someone dressed as a plague doctor, along with a speech bubble that contains marshmellows over a bonfire. There is a red circle with an X over this speech bubble, along with several frowney faces. &quot;Can you spot the snake in this picture, Doctor Graham? Maybe it's hiding in plain sight...&quot; Toby asked with playful sarcasm, though Trevor caught a glimpse of vengeful intent behind Toby's bright eyes.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor winces in sympathy. &quot;That's...just not mature,&quot; he says, looking at it closely for elements that don't make sense.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor remembers that Harley Sawyer is the daughter of two other doctors who pressured her to excel in their field from an early age. Her grandparents in turn were doctors, and so forth for multiple generations stretching back multiple centuries. It is possible that one of Harley's patients saw a plague doctor mask inside the Heroine's office, which might be a family heirloom with supernatural properties related to healing or disease.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Three guesses, and the first two don't count, doctor,&quot; Toby said. &quot;Hint: her surname rhymes with fun-slayer!&quot; Toby seemed to become increasingly upset the more he pressed Trevor about this topic. There was something personal about it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...Sawyer. Is there <i>anything</i> that woman can't be a perpetual high schooler about?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Bingo! Now that we're on the same page, let me be perfectly clear,&quot; He leans in to whisper in a conspiratorial manner. &quot;That pesky <i>Heroine</i> is preventing me from doing my job properly. Do you have <b>any</b> idea of how many artistic therapy classes require <b>scissors</b> for collages? It's not like I can just choose what goes into the rehabilitation curriculum, and I <b><i>despise</i></b> being pushed away from those who need me. My shadow has been getting leaner, doctor. I want to give Harley Sawyer the fright of her life, and I want my lesson for her to be stuff of Grimm's Fairy Tales. If you help me get rid of the Anathema she placed on me, I'll get in touch with my Kin who can find anyone who has been lost or taken, Ironside or Hedge. What do you say?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...sounds like a plan. And I need her off my back when I'm trying to look for Victor anyway.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Wonderful! I want you and I to invite Harley to the Six Flags down in Gurnee, and to tell her we're bringing Susie along to have all the fun she wants against Harley's so called medical orders. But the twist is that Susie will actually be one of my goblin servants in disguise. When Harley tries to pick a fight with us about being disrespected as a doctor, use you powers to make her horribly sick and nudge her to think it is a rare type of fever from the stress of all work and no play. At that point, I'll attack her with a Nightmare that'll make her think the goblin servant's ice cream will cure her disease, but inside it is a little something I can use to take her into my Lair when she falls asleep next. And she won't be able to fight that fever for long, will she?&quot; Toby's whispering became melodious and phantasmagorical, and Trevor had the impression only he could hear it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Heh...maybe we should also make her think she was targeted by the cult for getting involved but was focused on us, if possible?&quot;, a vicious look on Trevor's face. &quot;Might be hard to fake some writing, but I am *not* pleased with her right now, either.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;I'm an artist, and a gifted one at that, if I do so say myself! I can make an imitation of that kind of writing effortlessly. Once Harley learns her lesson and stays out of our way, we can focus on working together to find Victor and bring him back to Saint Helen's. And if he is indeed inside the Hedge... I'd bring Luke and his motley if I were you.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Oh yes, definitely.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;If you have any questions about the plan, now would be a good time to ask them. Otherwise, I need to get going.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;One major one - how are we going to excuse her absence? She sounds like she might be gone for a bit.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Simple! I'll be dropping her off in the edges of Grant Park, near Andrew Franklin's home. You know, the one with the vintage jukebox and mushroom garden? If we scare Harley masterfully enough, she'll be such an utter wreck that navigating through that place will be the last straw. She'll come running back to her clinic, covered in muds, sticks and bugs! Imagine that, what a pitiable and laughable sight. And best of all, if she tries to claim we are responsible for her fever, nobody will take her seriously because she'll look like a newbie camper who got drunk and lost a bet! Anyone who runs through the woods can scrape their skin on something dangerous.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Toby insists on driving Trevor and the goblin pretending to be Susie to the Six Flags. On the way there, the heat makes &quot;Susie's&quot; fingers melt off like wax, only for them to grow back with a squelching noise and the smell of something burning. Besides that, the goblin was a perfect imitator, and even asked <i>'are we there yet?'</i>, <i>'do they have unicorns?'</i> and <i>'I hope the other kids like playing the same games as me.\u2019</i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">With stern punctuality, the elderly and imposing Harley Sawyer does show up like she agreed to, but she is accompanied by a group of three unamused policemen, two concerned health inspectors, and one very reluctant teenager with a prosthetic leg. Harley is giving the teenager a genuinely protective look, one hand placed on his shoulder, and the other hand firmly grasping a walking cane. She is offering him the best motherly smile she can partially muster, which quickly dissolves back into a contemptuous stiffness when she meets gazes with Trevor.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Sir, Doctor Sawyer gave us information about your recent actions that require your arrest,&quot; One of the policemen said with a hand already on his nightstick, and gestured toward the health inspector who was holding a manilla envelope. &quot;Me and my coworker assembled a document in this envelope that contains Doctor Sawyer's most credible allegations, and Timothy is here as our witness of your medical malpractice. You better get over here and read what's on it while you still have the chance, Doctor Sawyer wants to take you to court and is not backing down,&quot; One of the health inspectors said as he stood there, taking a drag on his cigarette and groaning something under his breath about not-so-professional rivalries.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Susie&quot; ate her ice cream and hummed a slow little ditty from her home deep within the Thorns.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Toby played with a yo-yo made of bones and tendons as if he did not have a care in the world.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Timothy's gaze lingered around the amusement park, incapable of looking Trevor in the eyes.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>Okay. Act scared. </i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;R-really now? For what?&quot; He gives what he hopes to be a &quot;hidden&quot; &quot;dirty&quot; look. Keep Hawley thinking that she thwarted a plan...</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;For many reasons, and if I list them all, we'll be here the entire day, and I'm a busy professional who respects the time of her patients,&quot; Harley Sawyer said with an impassive face and a clinical tone. &quot;The children under your care have often suffered injuries and diseases due to your so-called <i>'extra-curricular activities'</i> that exposed them to the wilderness' predatory animals and poisonous plants. Timothy is here to confirm he lost his leg after it was bitten by a snake in one of your idiotic, unsafe excuses of learning experiences. And by the way, since he's been transferred to MY care after being adopted from Saint Helen's Orphanage, I took it upon myself to pay for his prosthetic leg and warn him to not be so trusting of people like you if he knows what's best for his health.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Timothy struggles to meet gazes with either Harley or Trevor. &quot;...It wasn't really his fault... I got too excited and ran off... he did do the amputation that saved my life, doesn't that count for something?&quot; Trevor remembers that when Timothy ran off on that day, the orphan accidentally stepped into a fairy ring within the wilderness and found himself lost inside the Hedge. &quot;He does warn us about the dangers, some of us just don't listen. I know another person from Saint Helen's that ate some weird berries while Trevor was with us. He got really sick for a week, but the only really bad part is that he had to redo some school exams, which he passed. And he was used to leaving everything for the last minute beforehand. I figured getting sick is part of knowing what keeps you healthy, Doctor Sawyer.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor gives a genuine smile. &quot;Thanks,&quot; he says to Timothy. &quot;And while I would prefer that the sickness doesn't escalate beyond nausea...&quot; He shrugs. &quot;You'll see I kept within ethical guidelines, too.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>&quot;What</b> ethical guidelines? And what about Susie? She was recently diagnosed with a cold, and yet here she is with you and the mustached clown, eating ice cream and risking contagion of the other children!&quot; She approaches &quot;Susie&quot; with a steely gaze and leaden steps, the wax-hobgoblin starting to sweat. &quot;Why did you accept his offer despite the medical orders I gave you? Is all of this just a childish attempt to disrespect me where everyone can see it?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The wax-hobgoblin's left pupil moves out of synch with the right pupil as if suffering from strabism, and &quot;Susie&quot; points at the health inspector who is smoking. &quot;Why don't you let me have fun and go look at his lungs instead, grandma?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Sawyer gives a death glare to the wax-hobgoblin, whose profuse sweat begins to smell strange and strained grin begins to melt. &quot;What on Earth is wrong with this girl, Trevor? Look at her, she might be suffering from a stroke because of you!&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Forcing his face to remain neutral, Trevor shrugs. &quot;Honestly? It's normal. She has had some...issues...&quot; Out of sight, he makes a quiet &quot;tone down&quot; gesture at the Hobgoblin.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">She scoffs, but ceases to interrogate &quot;Susie&quot; and turns her attention to Trevor, her walking cane pressed against his chest and keeping him out of touching range to afflict Harley with supernatural sickness. &quot;What's your goal? I'll have you know that I can tell at a glance when I'm dealing with someone who should never be a doctor. Shall I tell Susie about how you failed to prevent Victor from being kidnapped? Will she still trust you after she understands that she might suffer a worse fate than Timothy for treating you like her parent?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">...he can't help it. &quot;I'll cross that bridge when I come to it. Besides, that's not the question here and now, Hawley.&quot; He shrugs.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;What IS the question, charlatan? Do you think I can't take some juvenile banter without breaking down in tears, is that it?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Mostly? If she's the only one suffering some heat stroke from overwork. Like, you have no idea how badly stress aggravates some...issues. Makes people quicker to anger.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">And he unleashes his various internal toxins-and it feels like he just touched the sun.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">As he attempts to transfer his internal toxins to Harley, his hand partially melts into black and green slime with a revolting (squelch) noise. Harley uses her walking cane to brutally smack the melting hand away from her face, which is followed by a loud (crack) noise. Trevor's hand now looks like he shoved it into a furnace then hit it countless times with a meat tenderizer.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;You are absolutely <i>degenerate</i>, you are no better than <b>vermin</b>, and the only way to get rid of your <b><i>filthy</i></b> influence is QUARANTINE!&quot; As she yells that last word, the rest of Trevor's body rapidly melts into the same black and green slime, and his head is replaced by that of a zombie cat with the gaping jaws of a serpent and one eye dangling from the optic nerve.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Blisters full of pus spread throughout his skin and burst into clouds of obese flies, his bestial, unnatural hissing not quite that of a tiger or an anaconda. There is a horrid moment of shocked silence from everyone in the amusement park before the uncontrollable vomiting, screaming and running breaks out. &quot;SEE? HE'S NOT HUMAN, TAKE HIM DOWN!&quot; Harley commands the policemen, who hesitantly draw their pistols to avoid getting anywhere near Trevor now that his Horror Form has been revealed by Harley.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Well then. Time to sow confusion - or even better, take down Hawley with him in a way that everyone thinks it was hysteria. <i>&quot;...Am I, Hawley? <b>Am I?&quot;,</b></i> the great Alley Cait Sith hisses. <i>&quot;I am a diseased creature, it's true. I try to practice cleanliness. But tell me - I remember Meredith. Remember her, Hawley? Remember that little girl who used to play in the park, collecting nettles because she thought the plants looked lonely.&quot;* He gives a pained chuckle, not necessarily physical. *&quot;Remember how it was YOU who took her off her allergy meds? Because you felt they were 'keeping her reliant' on me? When I wasn't even involved, like I told you? She's mute now - I had to CUT OPEN HER THROAT with a CLAW just to get around her anaphylaxis! All because of you - but I wasn't kidding about the heat stroke, ma'am. You might see things THAT REMIND YOU OF WHAT YOU DID!&quot;</i> And he unleashes his full horror into her mind.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The sheer force of the dream-venom he patterned on Luke bowls through the surface of the sun he burned himself on, in fact using the very metaphorical fires themselves to ignite a very large, toxic gas plume in her mind, an oil fire of fear and insecurity that rushes through her brain and smears her synapses with tar and saliva. It's said that tiredness has similar effects to drunkenness, and while the encroaching dream has more in common with pathogenic bacteria than alcohol, they strangle coherent thought all the same. She knows the source of the rot - but she can't stop the spread of its mycelium than she can stop the sun setting, almost forming a different, deranged neural architecture over her own, one that only exists to say what isn't there - and frequently, lying.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Was Meredith, throat still clothed in bloody bandages, always there, looking at her with hate and fear as she tried to wordlessly panic-wave at the officers to not hurt her feline friend who saved her, the only thing out of her mouth being a wet gurgle of torn vocal chords? Why was she still bleeding? It didn't matter - she was here, and clearly wanted to protect the monster from the person she blamed for taking her voice.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Harley's ironclad expression cracks in half and despair comes pouring out of her eyes in great torrents. &quot;MEREDITH! I'M SO SORRY, I DIDN'T MEAN TO! I WAS WRONG TO FORBID YOU FROM PLAYING WITH THOSE PLANTS! AND YOU <b><i>NEEDED</i></b> THOSE ALLERGY MEDS, IT WAS MY MISTAKE, NOT YOURS!&quot; Her face is soon drenched in painful tears as she tries to feebly reach for the hallucination of Meredith in search of forgiveness and understanding.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">She soon falls to her knees, unable to fight the sleep deprivation induced by Trevor Graham's Nightmare. Toby approaches her, makes eye contact and offers her the ice cream laced with whatever substance would let him drag Harley into his Lair upon falling asleep. &quot;He's got a point, you know! Maybe you should start some self-care to fix your bitterness with something sweet!&quot; Harley devours the ice cream, but cannot muster even half of a smile as she falls asleep while muttering broken sentences about being sorry and wanting to play too.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The policemen have their pistols drawn, the health inspectors have already fled, and Trevor's Horror Form is still perceptible to everyone who might still be in the amusement park. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;You might want to dodge into that shooting range over there, with the animal plushies as prizes. It could help you dodge suspicion for long enough that the cops can say they lost you! You have toys in your Lair, right?&quot; Toby whispered in that same phantasmagorical tone that only Trevor could hear.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Will do, and I do,&quot; he hisses, running off. &quot;Hate Anathema, hate Anathema, hate Anathema...&quot; Was this even Anathema? Doesn't matter, he needed to find safety for his arm first. Stashing his Horror away later.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">As he makes his way over, the Alley Cat Sith reaches out and examines the flow between the mortal world and the Dream - he always conceived of it as veins, with his alterations a temporary infection akin to a cold - symptoms obvious but temporary, an eldritch sneeze to be wiped away by the world. It took some doing - but then at the last moment, he found a prime vein, one close to the surface. A quick swipe, and the toys isle were infected with his little helpers - one blink, and some of the cute plushies were suddenly replaced with ones that might have once been cute, with broad smiles and eerily lifelike eyes, covered in dust and decay. Eyes that twitch as he approaches and point in the right direction with suddenly present claws.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor Graham hears one of the three policemen dragging Harley's unconscious body out of the amusement park, while the other two request backup on their radios and approach Trevor's hiding spot with guns drawn and legs trembling. Trevor has to think of a quick way to make the two policemen go away before their backup arrives and decide to spray him with a hail of bullets. As he looks around, he notices that the toys in shooting range have become visually indistinguishable from his Minions Lair Trait. Perhaps he can make use of his powers to draw attention to the Minions as part of a tactic to drive away the policemen?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Breathing heavily, Trevor fixates on the nearest officer and reaches out with a temporary limb towards a nearby pebble - a flexible tentacle of torn slimy cloth - and tosses it over his Minions to create a deliberately loud noise from them. Just long enough to get them to look at the strange spooky plushies.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The nearest officer cautiously approaches the spooky plushy of a snake, his pistol firmly pointed at it. After several seconds of what appears to be nothing happening, he lowers his guard with a nervous sigh. &quot;As if the sewers last week weren't bad enough, now we gotta deal with this bullshit?&quot; He turns away from the plushie snake for a moment to see if Trevor is still inside that stall, and he is contorted just tightly enough beneath the desk to go unnoticed visually, but his smell is still horrible enough to make the officer turn his head away to vomit. The plushie snake comes to life and takes the chance to crawl up the officer's pants and bite him in the leg.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">He screams in agony and falls immediately, his pistol bouncing away from him and landing inside the stall. The Minions all unite with glowing eyes and hissing fangs, their appendages of slimy felt dripping with all manners of deadly toxins. The officer uses his arms to crawl away from the Minions since his leg is no longer responding to his brain, and the Minions all point and laugh at his fear and pain. Soon enough the Primordial Pathway closes, and the bitten officer has to be escorted into the police car and taken to the nearest hospital. Upon the desk, Trevor finds a hastily written note from Toby, which says the following:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Wondrously horrific job, Trevor! Consider your end of the agreement to be fulfilled. I'll get in contact with my Kin and tell you what they find out about Victor's whereabouts. Be on the lookout for updates through your Lair in 7 days or less!&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">When Trevor emerges from the stall, he is still affected by the Anathema that reveals his Horror, but the amusement park is completely empty.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Here's hoping I'm around for it,&quot; he gurgles to himself. &quot;Wounded...and I need to get this Anathema out. Never pleasant.&quot; He looks over himself. &quot;How do I fix you...or is it just the arm...&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Using the last bit of flow from the Dream, he pumps a bit of energy from the Alley Cait Sith into his arm - the slime from his bleeding wounds clump together and congeal, as bits of torn flesh feed on the toxic effluvia, cells dividing at a superhuman rate as his paw and hand replaces lost function. With a <i>crack</i>, the final nerve slaps back into place, and his limb starts working normally again.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...so, it's lasting. Damn it...&quot; Muttering curses to himself, Trevor slinks into the shadows, and decides to start chasing a lead. If one of his kids was abducted, then that means it's likely another has been menaced. Because if the abductor senses weakness, it's likely they'll be back...and it's likely one of his kids is targeted. So, careful to not be seen, he crawls into St. Helen's inactive AC vent...</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">And ends up face to face with someone who was planning his own prank in there with water balloons. Why said kid is in there is beyond him, but poor Reginald nearly pisses himself from the warping felid body slithering through, and the was itself was too shocked itself to even nibble as he ran screaming - the other kids, having grown tired of his antics, naturally do not believe him, especially when they realize what he was doing in there...but that doesn't make the twisted feline form feel better. Reggie was a class clown, not a monster.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor Graham notices that Reggie is sulking in a corner, tears falling down his black eye he got from the other kids for one nasty prank too many. When Reggie notices Trevor approaching, he goes wide-eyed in terror again. &quot;P-p-p-p-please just leave me alone... I won't do it again, I promise! I just wanted to make someone laugh!&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Whoa! Easy...I'm sorry,&quot; Trevor says, flinching back. &quot;I'm not here for that. I'm here for someone else.&quot; He realizes he needs a cover story. &quot;I'm a friend of Dr. Graham, and someone very bad hurt him. And they might be after some other children too...I just want to hide here and see if they come back. I will be careful not to touch you - I won't tell on you if you won't.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Y-y-y-you can't fool me, I know you're Doctor Graham!&quot; Reggie manages to blurt you. His eyes dart around the room, and he quickly dives underneath a nearby table that doesn't really hide him physically, but seems to calm his nerves somewhat. &quot;L-l-l-listen... I know you're not a bad guy, but you're scary. And if someone is scaring you, they must be really, really horrible! What are these monsters like? Were they carrying briefcases with toys inside?&quot; He whispers conspiratorially, and gestures with his hand for Trevor to hide underneath the table as well.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Yeah, I never was a liar-wait, briefcases with <i>what</i> inside?&quot;, Trevor says, slipping under as well (and being good on his word to not touch.)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Toys! Like, made of gears. They were really different from anything I ever saw before, they seemed almost alive. They offered me a gear-dinosaur, and I almost accepted, but I realized it was weird they already knew I love dinosaurs. Someone must have told them, but who?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Yeah...that's definitely someone who is able to know things they shouldn't...or betrayed trust,&quot; he says, aloud. Internally, he's relieved, because that does seem like exactly the kind of thing needed to calm a ravening Horror, something he suspects might need to happen to regain his human form. &quot;...and that is helpful. Did you see the toys up close, or otherwise hear them described?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Yeah, they opened the briefcases to show me the toys. Each one had a wind-up key with a red jewel on it. I asked them if they were working for someone, and they just said they're helping a Miss Jane Smith. I asked who Jane Smith was, and they only told me that she's a toy-maker looking to adopt kids, and then refused to answer any more questions.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor recalls that red jewels could also be rubies, which are a common type of vessel for Glamour in bargains with hobgoblins in the Goblin Markets of Chicago. More specifically, it is used to pay hobgoblins specialized in luring away people to places in the Hedge where their deepest desires can be fulfilled as a sort of appetizer for more expensive deals.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">It is possible that one of the cult members is a hobgoblin in disguise and taking orders from Jane Smith to look for potential children to kidnap. Since Trevor has never heard of a Changeling in Chicago who actually goes by Jane Smith, it heavily implies it was a just a random name used to throw off Reggie.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Last but not least, Trevor recalls that Victor always loved birds, and that his recurring nightmare about being trapped in a birdcage might imply Jane Smith or some other member of the cult has been using Oneiromancy on him.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;That...is actually very, very helpful.&quot; Trevor nearly smiles broadly, then remembers what he currently looks like. &quot;Thank you. Sincerely.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;D-d-d-don't go hurting anyone who didn't do anything bad, alright? You're supposed to be the parent we never had, we're counting on you to be a good example for us. I trust you, don't let me down! I'm disappointed enough in myself already...&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Yeah. I'm not intending to. And...I'm sorry for scaring you. I'll make it up to you, somehow.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">With that, he slides back out, and rings up Toby. &quot;Hello! I would like to talk about that favor...yes, I am aware of how soon I am calling it in. But I both have a time limit, and I think I have found someone who would be a very large danger to not just my patients, but the fae set as well...&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Say no more, doctor! I'll send my assistants your way posthaste. Do you have a Hedge-Gate you'd like me to specify for my assistants? Considering the circumstances, you might want to be discreet and ask Luke to be your bodyguard.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor remembers that there is a Hedge-Gate connected to a specific region of Garfield Park Lagoon.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Ah...&quot; He thinks. &quot;I think the Garfield Park Lagoon one might be good. And that is a good idea.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">After Trevor and Luke make their way to Garfield Park Lagoon, and try several times to make a stone bounce across the surface of the water seven times, Luke sings a nursery rhyme:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>\u201cCarry me, carry me, to the lands I have never been</i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>Show me, show me, the miracles I have never seen</i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>Be they wondrous or be they wicked, I want it all</i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>From summer until winter, from spring until fall\u201d</i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The surface of the water shifts to show what is within the Hedge on the other side: a tree with two bat-like hobgoblins dangling upside down from the branches. The moon is full, the air is cold, and there is a heavy smell of blood in the air that seems to coming from a Trod next to the bat-hobgoblins.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Come on now... would a doctor be afraid of a little bit of blood? We can show you what you seek, just jump inside the water... it won't be night in the Hedge for much longer, and by sunrise we must leave you.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Not at all. Blood is kind of my thing,&quot; Trevor says, nodding.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor sees 3 Trods in front of him: </span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The one on the left leads to a Goblin Market, and he can distantly hear echoes of clinking coins and gems on the wind.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The one in the middle leads to a Hollow made of gears, in the shape of a music box similar to those of Victorian England.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The one on the right leads to a trail of recent blood, apparently left behind by a wounded animal of considerable size.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Hm...&quot; He sniffs at the blood before making a decision.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;I'm thinking the clockwork,&quot; he says to Luke, gesturing at the lockpicks in the belt around his...well, torso, currently. Cats do not have a great place on the hip for a belt. &quot;You have the rations, Luke? I'd carry them, but I have the climbing gear as well...and the water....and the compass.&quot; Not that a compass was the greatest help in the Hedge, but establishing what direction was north was a good baseline for shaping it into a direction that was &quot;back home&quot;.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;I have them, but I don't think lockpicking a Hollow is a good idea, especially one we never stepped inside and is made of gears. There could be all sorts of traps hidden inside the walls. I brought brass knuckles just in case I need to punch something made of metal, but that won't help us if a trap goes off and removes my arm. And considering the fey laws of hospitality, that might not even be the worst punishment in store for a trespasser. What if there is nobody inside, and you can't pick the lock? What then, doc?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Then we start looking for alternate entrances...and examining the grounds. Maybe double back then.&quot; He pauses. &quot;Though, your suggestion?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;The only thing I can think of is breaking a window, then leaving a note attached to a stone saying: <i>'we have been watching you, and will come for you soon'</i>. That's all you really need to shift the blame away from us without accusing an innocent person of trespassing. They'll probably think it was from their Huntsman or something.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...I like it,&quot; Trevor says with a hungry smile. &quot;Shall we?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">There are clockwork birds circling overhead, and they are staring at the items Trevor and Luke brought.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor looks up, noticing the birds...and gets very cautious. &quot;Seems we might have sentinels...&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">As Trevor and Luke approach the front door, they don't notice any obvious security measures. The locking mechanism appears normal at a glance. The front windows are all covered, and there is a note on the front door saying: <i>'will be back soon, please be patient. Beware of the clockbirds, briarwolves and teapots.'</i> Nobody seems to be home.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Hm...&quot; Trevor looks up to see if said clockbirds are still watching.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The clockbirds are still watching.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...do you think it's possible to distract them?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Sure, but be quick with that lockpicking. If these things eat my eyes, you owe me a new pair,&quot; Luke grabs a handful of pebbles, flies up into the air, and starts pelting the clockbirds with full force, immediately breaking one of them. The rest go after him, pecking and scratching all at once with shiv-like beaks and talons. Luke screams in fear and pain as his chitinous flesh is covered in gashes and dark green blood pours out.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The front door has been unlocked, revealing the interior of the Hollow. The clockbirds move away from Luke, and return to their perching spot atop the front door.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Fuck, shit, goddamn stupid motherfucking birds! DOC! Help a brother who's helping you and patch me up, come on!&quot; Luke begs, trying to not move too much as he lies down on the ground waiting for medical assistance.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor doesn't need to be told twice, his claws fully extending to reveal the full, disturbingly medical glory, threads of his cloth skin coming off as stiches he easily threads through them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Trevor notices that the beaks and talons of the clockbirds were covered in some sort of supernatural toxin, which was created by a Changeling whose reserves of Glamour are nearly at maximum. As he peers inside, he notices a lot of teapot-like mechanisms preparing liquid mixtures that smell faintly of storeroom tranquilizers.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Huh.&quot; He looks around. &quot;If you weren't abducting children, I think I'd like you...&quot; Trevor looks for anything feeding the teapots.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Besides a series of liquids with the same chemical formulas as the compounds used in the baked goods of the cult's pamphlet, Trevor discovers a tank of fey jellyfish that is hooked up to the various teapots. Curiously, he remembers that Harley once wrote a medical thesis on the effects of various toxins from sea life on the human body to prove it was dangerous to go to the beach without proper protection, including a species that looks disturbingly like the ones in the tank.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...Well that isn't a pleasant thought. Wonder if you're sentient,&quot; he says to the jellyfish. Half-expecting an answer.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The jellyfish do not respond.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;Well then, thank you for the answer...I think,&quot; he says, looking for anything that look like text notes.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">What Trevor finds is a pastel blue and pink notebook full of fancy calligraphy that gradually deteriorates over the passage of time. Many of the pages are covered in recipes for confectionaries, nursery rhymes, and tea horoscopes. Some pages suddenly change into mad scribblings that are nearly identical to various drawings made by children at Saint Helen's Orphanage who are suffering from severe separation anxiety, PTSD and similar mental conditions. One overarching topic in this notebook is starting a family and throwing the best birthday parties ever, often with lists of baby names scratched out. Last but not least, there is an empty heart locket with a gear-like shape attached to the notebook via a red string.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">&quot;...okay. This is starting to creep even me out.&quot; With that, he goes back to check on Luke.</span></div></div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-24T19:50:16-07:00","id":1539181,"title":"","content":"Part 2/2<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Luke is examining the walls, floor and ceiling while flying around in a paranoid manner, as if wanting to make sure there are no traps hidden anywhere. &quot;So, what did you find, doc? You look like you've seen the Devil himself, what gives? Aren't you supposed to be a boogeyman? What could scare you of all people?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Read this,&quot; he says, flatly, showing the book. &quot;We're dealing with someone I think is genuinely sick in a way I don't enjoy. I feel like I'm looking at Alice in Wonderland's psychological horror version.&quot;<br /> <br /> Luke looks at the notebook for five seconds. &quot;Oh God! This... this is...&quot; He decides to not comment any further, and respectfully puts the notebook back into its original spot. &quot;How are we going to even deal with someone this far gone?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Well, this is getting into containing any epidemic that will destroy as much of it can and then itself...quarantine. And that means understanding how they think.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;And how are we going to do that? Just squat in here and wait for them to come back? We can't exactly figure them out with just one conversation, if they are even willing to talk after we broke into their Hollow.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Yeah...I'm thinking we take this journal and leave, then we follow any leads we have from here.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;You got it, doc. I don't wanna stay here either, I'm getting the creeps after reading that journal. Do we go to a Goblin Market next and ask around? I feel like most of them hobgoblin merchants are only gonna be interested if information is their specialty, and those types tend to trade secrets for secrets.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Yeah...and I feel there might be not enough secrets we have that interest them, plus might put my patients at risk, anyway. What was that third path, again?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;There was a pretty big trail of blood. Looked recent too, like from earlier today.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Yeah. That's definitely something, even if it's an old trail. Shall we?&quot;<br /> <br /> Upon following the trail of blood, Trevor and Luke encounter a phantasmagorical entity that resembles a floating cloud of black and red scribbles. It holds a large paintbrush with one hand, and an empty birdcage with the other. The trail of blood was left behind by that large paintbrush, which the entity drags along the soil of the Hedge in an absentminded manner. It occasionally pauses for several seconds to stare at its empty birdcage and sing a mournful melody in the same voice as Victor from back when he'd stare at the birds outside Saint Helen's Orphanage, pick a fight with the other orphans and then cry himself to sleep.<br /> <br /> Seeing it, Trevor hangs low to the ground instinctively, trying to assess hostility.<br /> <br /> Trevor notices something that makes his hair stand upright and his blood run cold. Those are not only the typical mannerisms of a Hedge Ghost, but also disturbingly similar to the way Victor often behaved, right down to the attempts at catching and drawing birds as a way to represent his desire to be free from the orphanage and become a functional, happy adult who can go wherever he pleases and draw the cool things he sees throughout the world.<br /> <br /> Trevor remembers Victor suffering from depressive episodes, during which he'd express his emotions by biting into his own hands and painting birds, only to lament they were red instead of blue. Considering Victor's recurring nightmares and his recent inexplicable disappearance, and the clues gathered about this so-called Jane Smith and her cult, it is possible they have rescued Victor from a deadly situation in the Hedge, possibly even a Durance. If this is true, then this is Victor's Hedge Ghost created by the trauma of being taken from Saint Helen's Orphanage.<br /> <br /> Trevor's mouth hangs limp a bit, before he inhales. &quot;...Victor?,&quot; he says, a little hollowly.<br /> <br /> &quot;...Dad? Is that you, dad? Where did mom go? Is everything alright? You're going to be together, promise?&quot; The Hedge Ghost says in a confused, fearful tone in Trevor's general direction. It is not clear if this means Victor considered Trevor a surrogate father figure or not, nor does it explain who was Victor's surrogate mother figure.<br /> <br /> &quot;I'm...I'm here. Don't worry, I'm here,&quot; Trevor says, carefully, comfortingly. &quot;I've been looking for you...&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Why are the birds so scared of me? I don't want to be scary... I swear I don't do it on purpose, I want to be good... maybe I deserve to be alone,&quot; The Hedge Ghost laments. &quot;I'm not even that good at drawing the birds, and who's gonna want to teach me? I'm trapped, trapped, trapped...&quot; The Hedge Ghost repeats that last word as if going through a state of dissociation.<br /> <br /> &quot;Shh....sh, it's okay....follow my voice...&quot; Trevor all but croons, drawing on every bit of bedside manner he has.<br /> <br /> &quot;Trevor? I miss you, Trevor... can you help me draw the spooky cat you are on the inside?&quot; The Hedge Ghost pleads, and gestures with its paintbrush toward the nearest tree.<br /> <br /> He nods. &quot;Sure. Can we...talk, while we do that...?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;We can. What do you want to talk about? I don't remember much...&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Can I ask...why the birds are scared of you?&quot;, he says, edging in before asking about &quot;mom.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Maybe they think I'm gonna pick a fight with them too, like I do with the other kids... it's not fair! What did I do to deserve this? Why can't I have what everybody else has?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;It's okay...I understand. I understand it too. Does...mom try to stop them?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Mom is trying her best, but she's not right in the head either... I don't know if she's gonna take good care of me, but she's all I have... I don't wanna go back to the birdcage, I don't wanna be told that I should be scary...&quot;<br /> <br /> Trevor thinks to the book and the lab. &quot;...is this cage in a room with jellyfish and a lot of clocks...?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;No jellyfish... but there is a big metal bird with the face of a woman... there were a lot of toys with wind-up keys, but none of them could be a real friend, just an imitation...&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...was mom...trying to make them real friends?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;She tried many times, but it never worked... there were other kids in that place beyond the spiky trees... they were all taught to fly... one was turned into a cloud, another became a butterfly, and I...&quot; The Hedge Ghost suddenly goes dead quiet and gives a thousand-yard stare.<br /> <br /> &quot;Hey...it's okay. You wanted to draw me?&quot;, he says, gently, as his mind's gears go into overdrive, and he starts to fully process what kind of changeling he's dealing with - at best, they're a changeling....<br /> <br /> &quot;I do! Can you still turn into the spooky cat? I always loved the noise it made, it was so cool!&quot;<br /> <br /> He looks at himself, nods, and immerses himself in the flow of blood from Dream to Real again, so much easier to access in the Hedge, and makes an incision.<br /> <br /> The Hedge Ghost draws an approximation of Trevor's Horror Form, humming in a happy tone for the first time in ages. &quot;Do you like it? Is it scary, but in a cool way, like you?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...you know what? I think so,&quot; he says, meaning it. It is a bit cute...but if he had a choice, it's what he'd prefer it to look like when dealing with people he didn't want to terrify.<br /> <br /> Luke speaks up. &quot;Doc... there isn't much more you can do for a Hedge Ghost. We better try and find Victor and this Jane Smith. Knowing how the Wyrd works, I bet they're together in the Hedge somewhere high above the ground. But this whole situation stinks of Loyalist business. A big metal bird? A toymaker Changeling with a clockwork Hollow? A Durance inside a birdcage? I dunno about the big picture, but if we're dealing with a Loyalist... well, you better call on every ally you have, because I ain't fighting one of the Gentry, especially not in the Hedge. Yes, this includes a truce with Harley Sawyer. No, I don't care if you have beef with her, she works for Torchkeep and those guys are investigating Victor's disappearance too.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Yeah...I agree. And yeah, that too...&quot; Trevor nods. &quot;We can only hope we can find the living Victor, too. It's just...somber.&quot;<br /> <br /> With that, he starts writing out a note, attached to the journal; he doesn't try to sweeten Sawyer up beyond a perfunctory admittance he was trying to get away earlier and did it in the most violent way possible, but asks her to check out the journal: &quot;I think I have found the diary of the reason for the disappearances. Trust me, read this, and you'll realize - there's a far worse quarry than me. One that we both would like defeated, for everyone's safety.&quot;<br /> <br /> On the following day, Harley shows up at the outskirts of Saint Helen's, this time without anyone else accompanying her. To say that her expression is full of dry contempt is like saying that the visages of Arcadia are maddening. She pulls out Jane Smith's journal, points at it with a stiff finger as if making a stabbing motion, and says nothing but: &quot;Explain.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Well, you were right on this being supernatural - I found that in an otherworld, along with proof Victor was there. Trust me, there is some dark things I found in the otherworld I duck into. And this is a threat to <i>me</i> as well, to say nothing of my charges.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;What kind of dark things? You're going to have to be more specific if you want me to take you seriously, especially after the <b>'prank'</b> you and the mustached clown played on me. What guarantee do I have that this is not yet another elaborate scheme to humiliate and undermine me?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Because I found some cult literature as well,&quot; he says, showing one of the flyers. &quot;And this distinctly resembles that symbolism. Besides, if I wanted to prank you again - going by that exchange rate, I'd lose another tendon, I think. I've discovered respect, if I didn't already.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Respect? From <i>you</i>? Why? Everything you have done up until now only indicated that you hate my guts, and would sooner have me die of some horrible abnormal plague than do my best to treat my patients. If you truly respect me, you better provide me with something that proves it. Maybe entrust one of your charges to me and don't interfere in my treatment for once? You never know what sorts of strange diseases a monster like you might be carrying after visiting another dimension where fairy tales are real.&quot;<br /> <br /> Trevor hums. &quot;...I can do you one better. You want to see what kind of diseases are from the Hedge? How about I let you check over one of said charges...and I give you some material?&quot; He reveals a couple bits of Goblin Fruit, shining with a semi-metallic look. &quot;You want to see what kind of strange life is from that realm, here's a few fruit, especially one affected by that lab I found.&quot;<br /> <br /> She raises an eyebrow and looks at the Goblin Fruit with a mix of suspicion and curiosity. &quot;I'm sending Torchkeep's deadliest agents after you if this fruit is a disguised bioweapon,&quot; Despite her hesitation, she places the Goblin Fruit in her purse. &quot;So, just to be clear: you want me to go into the Hedge with you and these two associates of yours in order to rescue Victor? No more and no less?&quot;<br /> <br /> Trevor draws himself up, and being as sincere as he can, nods. &quot;Precisely. Because, as much as I hate to admit it...I need your help. My pride is tertiary at best.&quot;<br /> <br /> And it's with that he slinks off to the lodge...biting back a curse as he realizes the moon's gone out of position. No worries, though - he simply reaches out with his interior claw, one normally used to open up the &quot;vein&quot; of the Dream, and wills it to cut open the gap between Hedge and mortal coil - a tickling of the pore that causes the Hedge to grow into the soft place that is the gate. Weirdly, he encounters no resistance - midway though, it seems like the soft place is actively guiding him on opening it.<br /> <br /> Having opened the Hedge Gate and taken the route back to the clockwork Hollow, Trevor and his allies soon hear the sounds of what appears to be a birthday party coming from inside the Hollow. The clockbirds and briarwolves are nowhere to be found, but if there are residents inside, that means any attempts at breaking and entering will be noticed.<br /> <br /> &quot;Be careful&quot;, Trevor says. &quot;We had some bad experience with the guards earlier. So, keep an eye out for anything odd that might be an attack.&quot;<br /> <br /> Harley nods, her expression hard to read from behind her plague doctor mask, just like the one in the patient's illustration that Toby showed Trevor a while back. She is carrying a first aid kit, a Cold Iron dagger, a pistol, and a briefcase whose contents she described only as &quot;emergency countermeasures&quot;. Toby holds back a chuckle at Harley as he hums along with the music coming from inside the Hollow, and Luke puts on his brass knuckles with none of his usual enthusiasm for a fight.<br /> <br /> Internally, Trevor beats the snark back about how she calls him out for being creepy while wearing Plague Doctor Apparel, as he checks for signs anyone has come back to the Hollow.<br /> <br /> Trevor Graham quickly notices that there are many fey entities inside, though only two seem to be talking to each other for the most part. From his position, he can only discern a few fragments of a conversation and visuals of the interior:<br /> <br /> &quot;Why are you looking so sad? Is there something wrong with the party?&quot; A female voice that sounds like a hissing teapot can be heard, and whatever murmuring of guests was happening beforehand goes utterly silent as the attention shifts to someone else, who throws a dagger onto a wall.<br /> <br /> &quot;Why can't I ever visit the places I used to? I can make my own decisions now, isn't this what the party is all about?&quot; A male voice responds in an expectant, almost impatient tone, followed by the flapping of clockwork wings and the scraping of claws on a table. &quot;And you told me that you'd find a father for me. What's taking you so long?&quot;<br /> <br /> Trevor, as much as it's unneeded, motions the others to remain quiet and out of sight, as they all listen in.<br /> <br /> There is a prolonged silence inside the Hollow, followed by the abrupt noise of someone getting up from a chair and heading toward the front entrance. &quot;I'm going out, and you can't stop me, THAT's what I want for my birthday!&quot; The male voice from before shouts. The door opens, and Trevor sees a teenager dressed like an aviator and equipped with clockwork wings<br /> <br /> His hands and feet end in talons, his skin is deeply tanned, his arms are covered in long and thing scars, and he wears a badge of a blue jay on his leather jacket. He looks at Trevor like he is trying to unearth a comforting memory buried under a lifetime of horrific trauma. &quot;Wait... do I know you? You look... <i>very</i> familiar. And who are these three people with you?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;I don't...think so?&quot;, Trevor says. &quot;Mr...?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Victor. Am I supposed to know who you are?&quot; He asks. &quot;...did you ever work at an orphanage?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...I still do,&quot; he says, hoping his sudden discomfort doesn't show.<br /> <br /> As he does so, a bit of the Alley Cait Sith's distinct infectious slime leaks out as sweat, as everyone gets the sense of motion as its tail twitches nervously.<br /> <br /> &quot;Wait... you're not human! But you're not fey either, are you? That slimy cat head...&quot; He slowly approaches Trevor, wings unfurling and talons unsheathing. &quot;Trevor Graham? I'm not going mad, am I?&quot; He looks around, as if concerned that he is trapped in a dream. &quot;Tell me something only the real Trevor would know about me.&quot;<br /> <br /> Trevor remembers that Victor loved to play with paper airplanes as a kid, and named one of his paper airplanes after Trevor: &quot;The Slime Jet T-83&quot;. 1983 is the year Trevor was born, and Victor would use green paint and strands of his own hair to mimic Trevor's Horror Form when designing that paper airplane.<br /> <br /> Trevor perks up. &quot;...you made a paper airplane based on me. The Slime Jet T-83. Remember that?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;I do. How... how long has it been? I've barely left the Hedge since Gertrude adopted me, and I don't think it's helping me tell apart what is real from what isn't. Can I talk to you somewhere private? I don't trust these other people you brought with you, and we need to be far away from this Hollow so Gertrude won't get over-protective of me yet again.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Now listen here, Victor. If your adoptive mother has been neglecting your health, be it physical or mental, it is my sworn duty to help however I can. Even if that means knocking some sense into her head via invasive therapy procedures, and it seems like she isn't in the right state of mind to determine whether she can refuse medical assistance or not,&quot; Harley Sawyer half-offered, half-threatened. &quot;Move aside, let's not make this more difficult than it has to be.&quot;<br /> <br /> Luke and Toby look at each other, as if uncertain about what to say.<br /> <br /> &quot;I dunno, man. I just came here to bust heads if someone innocent gets caught in a dangerous situation,&quot; Luke shrugged. &quot;I am good at doing the opposite of healing folks.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Curiouser, and curiouser... perhaps we can go inside the Hollow and discuss Victor's psychological issues with Miss Gertrude like civilized people?&quot; Toby recommended, his conciliatory tone subtly betrayed by a small, wicked grin. &quot;I know how to measure my words around the unsound of mind.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Before we do anything - Gertrude?&quot;, Trevor asks. &quot;Is it it fine if I ask who she is before we go deeper with it?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;She's the one who rescued me after I escaped from a place she called Arcadia. She says that she escaped from it too, and went through the same nightmares I did. She looks like a clockwork doll and... well, she's doing her best to raise me while she doesn't find someone to marry, or so she says. I don't want to be stuck with someone so clingy when I can take care of myself. She tells me that life back in the human world isn't like living in the Hedge, and that it'd be ungrateful of me to just walk out after all she did for me.<br /> <br /> I don't want to only have talking teapots and dancing dandelions as my friends forever. I wanna go back to the human world, but maybe Gertrude is right that I'm not ready yet. She told me there are others like me and her, and that I can visit them once in a new moon, as long as I return to her Hollow so she can finish raising me.<br /> <br /> I did visit these other fugitives, and... I don't think I got along with any of them. I heard rumors that they hate me because Gertrude is keeping dangerous secrets and is using me as an infiltrator.<br /> <br /> What do I do? I want to keep flying no matter what, but I don't want to be stuck in the clouds everyday either. I started seeing weird things that aren't really there when I look at the clouds. I don't think the Hedge is supposed to be anybody's home forever, it's like everything here is only half-real. It's starting to make me question if I ever escaped at all. Maybe I'm still in Arcadia and... that <b><i>thing</i></b> is still watching me from above the clouds.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Hey, don't worry. You're actually out,&quot; Trevor says with a smile. &quot;Luke here is a changeling who adapted back home,&quot; he says with a look at Luke and Toby, &quot;while Toby's like me.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;And who is the woman in the weird outfit with a bird mask? She sounds like a complete know-it-all, and the Hedge always has something to surprise you,&quot; Victor said. &quot;Is she a human?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Pretty much,&quot; Trevor says. &quot;Dr. Harley, Victor, Victor, Dr. Harley.&quot;<br /> <br /> Victor hesitantly shakes hands with Harley. &quot;If we don't find a spot to talk in private soon, Gertrude is gonna come looking for me...&quot; Victor's wings and talons whirr and click in nervousness. There is a distinct air of reluctance in his body language, like he is holding back from doing something impulsive to get out of this situation.<br /> <br /> &quot;Seems for the best&quot;, Trevor agrees. &quot;Let's find a safe place.&quot;<br /> <br /> Victor spreads his wings, flies upwards at a staggering velocity, and plummets with talons drawn to grab Trevor and carry him off to the top of a tree with a huge nest on one of its branches. On this nest, there are various Goblin Fruit preserved inside jars, a diary with a locking mechanism, miniature clockwork airplanes, sacks of seeds, binoculars, a vintage radio, sleeping bag, and rifle bullets.<br /> <br /> After being set down, Trevor whistles. &quot;This is...very nice. Very fitting for you, too, I must say.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Thanks, Trevor. The parts and tools for those airplane models didn't pay for themselves, I can tell you that much. But I know my way around Goblin Markets, and even bought my own gun!&quot; He takes out a clockwork egg with a wind-up key from his pocket, and after a few turns of the key, it rapidly expands and shifts into an hunting rifle.<br /> <br /> &quot;It's a lot less heavy when I'm flying, and it can work with all kinds of ammo, even magical ones. How cool is that? Oh yeah, now that we're alone, tell me. How is everyone doing at Saint Helen's Orphanage? Nobody else got kidnapped, right? Because if they were, I'm going to hunt down whoever is responsible and kill them, no matter what Gertrude says.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Nice! And as far as I know, they are not vanishing...yet. But there is hints of a risk that the people who took you are still patrolling.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Who are these people, even? Why did they choose me to kidnap of all orphans?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Still figuring it out, but...were you drawn in by a pamphlet? Because I've seen a bunch of those...&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;One of them did show me a pamphlet... I remember drawings of cakes and tea, but I ripped the pamphlet in half for some reason. I remember getting into a fight before I was kidnapped, the door to my room opened on its own and thorny vines dragged me into the Hedge.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...Huh. Anything else you can recall? Like...who the fight was with?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;It was with a big mechanical spider, which caught me in its webs and was about to eat me, but then... the <b><i>thing</i></b> that held me prisoner in Arcadia swooped in and took me out of the webs. She was a big metal bird with the face of a woman, and she promised to make my dreams of flying come true.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...I see. That was...unexpected. But useful.&quot; Mechanical spiders - perhaps Gertrude was not directly linked? Or was that a hobgoblin?<br /> <br /> &quot;Trevor, you were always good with children, and I think Gertrude has some unresolved childhood trauma that goes really, really deep. Do you think you can help me convince her to let go of that pain so she can let go of me too?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...I can try. She seems like...she needs it.&quot; And strong competitor for &quot;understatement of the year&quot; has entered the contest...<br /> <br /> Victor takes Trevor back to the entrance of Gertrude's Hollow, and they find Harvey, Luke and Toby waiting there.<br /> <br /> &quot;So? What plan do you expect us to follow? I'll have you know that I am not willing to entertain the mental illness of whoever is inside that house,&quot; Said Harvey, her grip tightening around her metal cane.<br /> <br /> &quot;Will you just stop being such a <b>bitch</b> already? Trevor's the expert here, not you. Do you wanna take the blame if you force things to go your way?&quot; Critiqued Luke, his antennae twitching and mandibles clicking.<br /> <br /> &quot;I'll just be around if someone needs me to speak to the madwoman in the Hollow in a way she'll understand,&quot; Toby said as he checked his face's reflection on a handheld mirror. His Horror had leaked through his skin and taken on the form of a fey Mien akin to a teddy bear, complete with buttons replacing his eyes.<br /> <br /> &quot;Whoa, whoa! Easy, we already have enough issues,&quot; Trevor says, alarmed. &quot;Did anything happen while I was gone?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Just Harvey wanting to be a Heroine and do everything on her own, big shock,&quot; Luke grumbled.<br /> <br /> &quot;I see...&quot; Trevor sighs. &quot;But, if anything, I got some insights for what I believe is happening at the moment. Shall we save the argument for after I explain?&quot;<br /> <br /> Harvey goes silent and motions with her hand for Trevor to explain the situation.<br /> <br /> &quot;Okay. So, I think I met a Hedge Ghost cast off by Victor...&quot; Trevor gives a truncated version of what he learned there.<br /> <br /> &quot;...I'm sorry, man,&quot; Luke says in a hollow, broken tone in an attempt to comfort Victor. &quot;If it serves as consolation, I can help you join the Summer Court once this situation is over.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;It seems to me that this adoptive mother of yours has made previous attempts at kidnapping human children to build her family and assuage her separation anxiety. I'm not sure if she'll be receptive to psychological therapy, especially from strangers,&quot; Harvey observed in a clinical tone.<br /> <br /> &quot;Hear me out. Let's play along with the Wyrd. This person is a clockwork doll with a fondness of cake and tea, so why not announce ourselves as guests for a special tea party?&quot; Toby recommended. &quot;After all, Victor is an adult, and adults get to choose who their friends are.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;I mean...it could work, but we need to make sure we aren't walking into a trap.&quot; And, unspoken, he feels like he needs the food after what he lost in the very same fight that left him needing to regrow an arm.<br /> <br /> &quot;Then why don't we just ring the doorbell? If she's against receiving visitors right now, surely she will be loud and clear about it. Traps are for intruders, not visitors,&quot; Toby suggested. &quot;Seems to me that we're prevaricating around the Thorns for no good reason. We can handle whatever happens in there.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;I mean...&quot; Trevor thinks. &quot;That's true. I'm just also paranoid. Especially given those clockwork birds, she might not exactly enjoy unannounced visitors. And I'm not sure how to announce ourselves.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Trevor, listen here. You asked for my help, and I'm fine with that, but if you won't take the lead as the supposed expert, I'm going to ring that doorbell and you will all play along with whatever I choose to say to that kidnapper,&quot; Warned Harvey.<br /> <br /> &quot;Understood,&quot; Trevor says. &quot;And I'm thinking we could send a note, if nothing else.&quot; He hums. Clearly, hunger and worry makes him too cautious, and he knows it.<br /> <br /> Harvey scoffs at Trevor's proposal, and rings the doorbell.<br /> <br /> The clockwork doll who opens the door looks at Trevor, Harley, Toby and Victor, and gives them a seemingly genuine if lopsided smile.<br /> <br /> &quot;Well hello there, I don't think Victor has introduced me to the three of you before. What do I owe the pleasure? My name is Gertrude, and I can see you gentlefolk came from far away, you look exhausted,&quot; She extends a mechanical hand for the visitors to shake.<br /> <br /> Trevor, realizing they're doing it now, shrugs, and smiles, shaking her hand - might as well be the best guest possible. &quot;Trevor. Pleasure to meet you.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Ma'am, we're here to talk with you about the way you have been treating your son as a substitute for psychological therapy to fix your separation anxiety. We have been conducting investigations into your activities, and quite frankly, it is obvious you need urgent intervention in order to cease posing a threat to others and yourself. Victor is not your property, and you cannot shelter him from the dangers of life indefinitely just to have a paliative for your neuroses. As a medical professional, I'm hereby requesting that you allow Victor to come and go as he pleases, and stop all attempts at kidnapping people, especially if it involves the use of supernatural abilities that place their physical and mental health at risk,&quot; Harley imposed.<br /> <br /> &quot;...And why should I listen to you? Victor is doing fine with me, it seems to me that the only kidnappers are in front of me,&quot; Gertrude rejected. &quot;Are you trying to take him away from me? I'll have you know that I am perfectly capable of dealing with anyone who threatens my son!&quot; Gertrude maintains her smile, though now it has taken on a distinctly unhinged appearance.<br /> <br /> &quot;Not at all our intention!&quot;, Trevor says, cutting in. &quot;We're trying to help him - and helping you have a bit more energy for yourself in the future as well as him. We fully understand how you feel.&quot; Truly, it was a weird day when a Beast was Good Cop.<br /> <br /> &quot;What do you mean when you understand how I feel? Feel about what?&quot; Gertrude seemed slightly less defensive and jittery, but still wary and ready to fight.<br /> <br /> &quot;About...well, what it's like to have\u2026 what you considered just and normal taken from you. And being forced to make accommodations due to that.&quot; Trevor winces, rubbing his arm.<br /> <br /> &quot;...What was normal and just is forever lost to me, it's lost for every member of my kind. How could you possibly help me feel any better about a wound that can't be healed?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;I get it. But, the truth is...that's how a Beast feels a lot, too. And I have to fill that hole with terror of others - I don't like how, as a doctor, I need to feed on the fear of infection, age, infirmity...I need to eat the things I am meant to help treat. I have to keep it open, because for me...it's a maw. No matter how much I'd like to be human, I really can't be.&quot;<br /> <br /> Gertrude gives Trevor a look of disbelief, but with a hint of curiosity. &quot;Trust me, I tried all sorts of pharmaceutical solutions, and I don't think any of them worked like I hoped. I just... want to give someone else the happy life I didn't have...&quot; She starts rubbing her arm and averting her gaze, the gears in her body whirring and clicking in a slow, heavy pace. It seems there is something she isn't revealing about herself.<br /> <br /> Trevor frowns, and peeks over - directly at the right angle to make a full assessment, by sheer luck.<br /> <br /> Gertrude is conspicuously avoiding any explanations about who exactly is supposed to be Victor's adoptive father figure.<br /> <br /> &quot;...can I ask who Victor's adoptive father is? We have been talking around it...and I get the sense you might feel uncomfortable. I will not judge, I just want to hear the full story for both yours and his sake.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...I have been looking for one, but I've yet to find someone who I feel that I can trust. My frustration led me to look for other ways, and I've found out that maybe I can create one if I look carefully enough through the Goblin Markets for... parts.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...ah?&quot; Trevor frowns. &quot;Like...a Fetch?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Exactly like a Fetch, doctor. The problem is that only one kind of being knows how to create Fetches... and dealing with them hasn't exactly done wonders for my reputation,&quot; She adds in a conspiratorial whisper, as if fearing that Victor might hear what she's saying. &quot;I'm close to completing the construction of this person, and after that I'll finally have a happy ending.&quot;<br /> <br /> Luke holds back a cynical chuckle, Harvey pinches her nose in frustration, and Toby sips his tea with an unreadable facial expression. Gertrude eyes the three of them in an offended and wounded manner.<br /> <br /> &quot;...I see.&quot; He thinks. &quot;And...the reason They won't take Victor? Or you?&quot;, he whispers back, trying to let her remain comfortable.<br /> <br /> Gertrude goes suddenly silent, and her silence is deafening. Her gaze reaches a thousand yards, her body language is petrified, and Trevor can hear the clock-birds approaching a closed window of the Hollow. They tap their metal beaks against the glass, as if awaiting orders from Gertrude.<br /> <br /> There is a knock on the front door of the Hollow. The voice outside seems to be that of Victor.<br /> <br /> Trevor shoots up. &quot;...Everyone. Form defensive positions,&quot; he says to the others, turning around slow. And speaking up. &quot;Hello...Victor?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Hello, Trevor! I saw the clock-birds trying to get inside! Let me in so I can talk some sense into Gertrude!&quot; The winged Changeling shouts.<br /> <br /> Luke, Harvey and Toby assume defensive positions.<br /> <br /> The windows of the Hollow begin to open on their own via a series of clockwork mechanisms, and Gertrude slowly walks backwards to reach inside a drawer.<br /> <br /> Gertrude conjures a sword from the drawer, its blade glistening with an acrid-smelling and sickly-green liquid. The windows open and the clock-birds start cawing in threatening unison. Victor notices that the front door locked itself after Gertrude snapped her fingers, and blows out the doorknob with his hunting rifle and kicks open the door.<br /> <br /> There is so much of Trevor that, furious with Gertrude, wants to pounce on her. To strangle her. To make her pay.<br /> <br /> But...Victor wanted to talk. And to not do so seemed...wrong. Because right now...he was trying to take care of Victor.<br /> <br /> &quot;Hey, hey! There's been enough violence for one day! Please, put the blade DOWN!&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;GERTRUDE! These people are trying to HELP you, and THIS is how you repay them? How can you pretend to be mentally well enough to raise me when this is how you react to anyone who criticizes your parenting?&quot; Victor points the hunting rifle at Gertrude, his arms shaking and sweat forming on his forehead. &quot;Do as he said, put down the sword! We can talk this out like sane people!&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Lady, listen to your son, already! Does he look like he wants to be forced to shoot you before you do something crazy?&quot; Luke said as he raised his brass knuckles. &quot;If you wanna fight, that's fine by me, but don't bitch about it being unfair later!&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;If you don't release Victor, I'm going to act in legitimate defense of another, and I will NOT hold back!&quot; Harvey said, and she reached for her Cold Iron dagger and chemistry flask.<br /> <br /> &quot;Well, things went from playtime to family drama rather quickly,&quot; Toby said, and locked gazes with Gertrude. &quot;You better hope the others knock some sense into you before I do,&quot; He added in a coldly mischievous tone.<br /> <br /> Gertrude shoves her fingers into her mouth to release a loud mechanical whistle, and one of the clock-birds dives inside the Hollow like a lightning bolt. &quot;If Trevor is going to break his promise to me, then I suppose he must be reminded of what a TRULY bad guardian is like!&quot; The clock-bird returns with an Auroch's Horn, which Gertrude sounds without a moment of hesitation.<br /> <br /> &quot;....FUCK! We need out of here, _now!&quot;_ Trevor picks up victor, and starts running out. &quot;The Gentry are coming! Or Huntsmen! Either is <b><i>bad!</i></b>&quot;<br /> <br /> There is split second of horrible, deadly silence as the combatants all prepare to make their moves.<br /> <br /> Harvey leaps toward Gertrude, Cold Iron dagger already drawn and aiming for the jugular with surgical precision. Gertrude dodges sideways, just barely avoiding a decapitating blow.<br /> <br /> Gertrude counter-attacks with her poisoned sword, only for Harvey to grab a nearby chair and use it as an improvised shield. The poisoned blade becomes stuck, and Gertrude is forced to release it.<br /> <br /> Luke transforms into a swarm of wasps and envelops Gertrude with an unrelenting series of agonizing stings. Gertrude attempts to fight the swarm back with a broom in a fit of desperation, but it doesn't work. Gertrude is now blinded by the swarm and vulnerable to an attack.<br /> <br /> Swearing, Trevor unleashes his full might of Nightmares at Getrude. &quot;If you wish the Huntsmen so bad - you can stay with them!&quot; In a bit of inspiration, he wills sleep to take her.<br /> <br /> Gertrude immediately falls unconscious, her mechanical body covered in puncture wounds that leak oil as if it was blood. Victor is hyperventilating, and grabs Trevor with a sense of unspeakably extreme urgency.<br /> <br /> &quot;Did you... did you hear that?&quot; Victor asked, his body almost entirely petrified by sheer terror.<br /> <br /> There was the sound of wind chimes carrying the cries of kidnapped children.<br /> <br /> There was the sound of a great metallic bird rising from a mountain and taking flight.<br /> <br /> There was the sound of a thunderstorm as the previously clear skies became dark as night.<br /> <br /> &quot;Sounds like something we don't want to be here for.&quot; He hugs Victor. &quot;Let's get out of here. Hedge?&quot;, he asks the other. &quot;Or just the street?&quot;<br /> <br /> Victor guides the others to a hidden hatch beneath the soil, far beyond Gertrude's Hollow. &quot;I've been working on this for a long time whenever Gertrude was distracted, because I knew one day I'd have to escape from her fast,&quot; Beneath the hatch is a staircase leading to a small hangar, with a WW2-esque airplane on display. &quot;No use running away on foot if the enemy can fly, we need to play the same game if we want the Wyrd to give us even half a chance of making it out of here alive! Go on, get inside, all of you, and <b><i>NO FUCKING BICKERING!\u201d</i></b><br /> <br /> Trevor makes sure to set up Victor up in a seat first.<br /> <br /> Try as Victor might, the thunderstorm is making it almost impossible to properly pilot his airplane. His face turns pale as his grip on the controls tighten, and he briefly glances toward Trevor for a word of reassurance, any word.<br /> <br /> There in the horizon, an otherworldly, winged figure approaches, its shadow cast on the thunderclouds but its face beyond sight. Its cry echoes through the atmosphere like an impending doom inside a nightmare.<br /> <br /> Trevor hisses. &quot;...Here we go. Victor, you have this,&quot; he says, with as much comfort as he can manage. &quot;Any way to help shake this thing from my end? Perhaps with my powers?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Fuck if I know!&quot; Victor screams in frustration and horror. &quot;Oh God, oh GOD, it's getting closer!&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;If all else fails, can't you enter your Lair and bring us with you?&quot; Toby suggested, his eternal cheerfulness finally cracking under pressure.<br /> <br /> &quot;Yes, but I need to...&quot; He pauses. &quot;Wait. Wait! Hold a second, this very much is not a betrayal-&quot; He leans out. &quot;Lord Thunderbird! Can you hear my cry?! I know where the one who called you is!&quot;<br /> <br /> A mimicry of a regal woman's voice rings out from the heavens, its laughter immaculately mechanical.<br /> <br /> &quot;A cat making a desperate offer to a bird... how peculiar... you have my curiosity. Do not waste it.&quot;<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;Lady</i> Thunderbird, apologies! The one known as Getrude - she has betrayed all of us and broken a promise she made. I also understand that Contract she invoked was against your knowledge, and must have been humiliating to need to follow - I will give you knowledge of where she is, and promise to never seek her again, if you allow this plane a chance to escape with all passengers!&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;The clockwork doll, yes... no better punishment for a selfish mother than having no one to care for except her wicked self... no husband, no child, and no hope of morning... you are more like me than you know. We enjoy the same pleasures, my fellow monster. I will let you go, a life in exchange for another life. This Pledge is now sealed. Go back to Ironside, and use your freedom wisely. Gertrude sounded the Auroch's Horn, and I am still owed the lives of seven children more. I look forward to crossing paths with you again.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...your approval is...most appreciated, my lady...&quot; In that he appreciates what he just did...and hates the fact his Horror is purring.<br /> <br /> The True Fae leaves them be, and plunges downward to drag Gertrude back to Arcadia. Victor just barely manages to land his airplane next to the nearest Hedge Gate back to Chicago.<br /> <br /> &quot;...what now?&quot; Victor asked, his face marked by a thousand-yard stare.<br /> <br /> &quot;Well, I promised to introduce you to the Summer Court, and I still plan on doing that. It'd be wrong to leave you alone after... all of this,&quot; Luke said, and shook hands with Victor.<br /> <br /> &quot;Well... tell me one thing, Harley. Since you have taken it upon yourself to fight monsters, would you say Victor deserves to be called a monster? He's part fey now, so you have to choose where you will draw a line,&quot; Toby pointed out, and Harvey remained silent for several seconds.<br /> <br /> &quot;...Victor is still dealing with the aftermath of a lot of trauma. I'll be patient with his mistakes, but I expect Trevor to act as a worthwhile parental figure while Victor sorts his own life out. I'm not made of stone, even if nobody believes that.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...thank you.&quot; Trevor pauses. &quot;...and I promise. I'll help him avoid being as much of a monster as I can be...&quot; He looks back to where the Hedge Gate was, and shakes his head.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-05T12:17:32-08:00","id":1556182,"title":"","content":"Excellent! I love the whole story, so very, well, fairytale in nature. Plus, it shows antagonists need not be malevolent, merely opposed","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28921-creatorfoundermaker","name":"CreatorFounderMaker","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"91"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}